---
import Headshot from "../components/Headshot.astro"
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro"
---

<Layout title="About">
  <Headshot size={280} class="about-headshot" />
  <p></p>
  <h3>Hello!</h3>
  <p>
    I'm Christian Danielsen, a web developer working remotely in beautiful
    Oregon.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some things I'm into: JavaScript/TypeScript, the art of writing tests,
    continuous everything, and working on teams with a diverse group of people.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here are some of my{" "}
    <a href="/resume.pdf" target="_blank">
      credentials
    </a>
    . Please feel free to{" "}
    <a href="mailto:ckdanielsen@gmail.com?subject=Ahoy, Christian!">
      get in touch!
    </a>
  </p>

  <div>
    <h3>(In)Frequently Asked Questions:</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Q: HotMess?</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      A: I hiked the <a href="https://www.pcta.org">Pacfic Crest Trail</a> in
      2010. On a long trail, it's a tradition to adopt an alter ego (a trail
      name). My propensity for misplacing things landed me the title of
      'HotMess'.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Q: What's with the masthead?</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      A: If you work with JavaScript, you've probably encountered the famous{" "}
      <a href="https://www.destroyallsoftware.com/talks/wat">wat talk</a>,
      detailing some of JavaScript's best... eccentricities. I'm so enamored
      with the masthead expression (which evaluates to true in JS), that I'm
      considering getting a tattoo of it.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  :global(.about-headshot) {
    display: block;
    margin: 0 auto;
    width: 17.5rem;
    height: auto;
  }
</style>
